course: dictionary
lesson: 1965
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists them as numbered senses, and the context tells you which one fits. Take «competition»: it can be an event, a situation, or the people you are racing against.
explain: «Competition dancers keep their arms still and move their feet very fast.» Here competition means an organised event where dancers try to win. Ask yourself: is this a thing that happens, or a situation? The clues tell you which.
explain: «There are spelling competitions and sports competitions at school.» Competitions with an s means more than one event. «Animals and plants are in competition for food, light and space.» Here competition means the situation of trying to get more than others.
fact: Competition has three main meanings: an organised event, the situation of trying to be better than others, and the people you are competing against.
fact: «A competition» is an event you can enter and win, such as a race, a quiz or a contest.
fact: «Healthy competition» means friendly trying your best, and «tough competition» means strong rivals.
fact: You can «enter a competition», «win a competition» and be «in competition with» someone.
fact: Check the meaning by putting your choice into the sentence: if you can enter or win it, it is an event.
exercise: «In the spelling competition, Mia spelled every word right.» In this sentence, competition means | The situation of being better than others | The people you are competing against | *An organised event where people try to win
exercise: Shops are in competition for customers. What does competition mean here? | *The situation of trying to be more successful than others | An organised event with a prize | A shop with a big sale
exercise: Which phrase means taking part in an event to try to win? | Healthy competition | Free phone | *Enter a competition
test: «Competition dancers keep their arms still and move their feet very fast.» Here competition dancers means | dancers who only dance alone at home | dancers who stop moving to rest | *dancers who take part in events to try to win
test: «Animals and plants are in competition for food, light and space.» Which meaning fits best? | *The situation of trying to get more than others | Free food from a shop | An organised event with a prize
exam: «You've won a free phone! when you entered no competition» is a scam. Here competition means | *An organised event you can enter | A fight between two people | A kind of phone | Something that is free
